    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d163">

                <pb facs="0295" n="233" sameAs="frus:frus1878/pg233"/>

                <head>No. 158.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Evarts to Mr. Taylor</hi>.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1878-10-19"><hi rendition="#italic">October</hi> 19,
                        1878</date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 29.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Your dispatch of the 5th July last (No.
                    28), in relation to the case of Mr. Willimek, has been received.</p>
                <p>The department has received with satisfaction the information conveyed by you in
                    relation to the course pursued by the Imperial Government of Germany with regard
                    to naturalized German-Americans who may have occasion to revisit their native
                    country. The substance of that information has been communicated to Mr.
                    Willimek, who will no doubt avail himself of the immunity thus secured to him by
                    the generous policy of the German Government.</p>
                <p>I am, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>WM. M. EVARTS.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
